

:: day_4_38.1_les

The windows became a calendar.

Not gradually — not a gentle fade or a polite transition. One moment I was staring at Eidolon City's amber-hazed skyline, the next the entire floor-to-ceiling span of smart-glass flickered and reorganized into a massive holographic display that stretched from wall to wall. The city was still visible beneath it — ghosted, dimmed to a murmur of neon — but the foreground now belonged to a horizontal band of color that unspooled across the glass like a ribbon pulled from a spool.

Twenty-eight segments. Twenty-eight days. Each one a vertical bar shading seamlessly into the next, forming a gradient that told a story I didn't want to read.

Cool blue on the left — pale, almost silver, calm. It deepened into cerulean through the first cluster of days, then began to warm. Teal. Seafoam. A brief, shimmering band of warm rose at the center — vivid, almost luminous, a single day that glowed brighter than anything around it. Then the descent: rose cooling into mauve, mauve darkening into violet, violet thickening into plum, plum bleeding into a deep, bruised purple that compressed at the far right edge before collapsing into —

Red.

Not bright red. Not cheerful. A dark, heavy, arterial crimson that occupied the final four to five days of the cycle and pulsed with a slow, rhythmic throb that synchronized with the neon veins in the walls. The apartment's ambient lighting shifted to match — a barely perceptible reddening at the edges of my vision, as if the room itself was demonstrating what those final days would feel like from the inside.

<<system "Module 6: Menstrual Cycle Simulation." >>

<<system "The user's avatar operates on a complete 28-day hormonal cycle simulated by the VR engine for full physiological immersion." >>

<<leona "No.">>

<<system "This cycle governs mood, energy, appetite, skin condition, breast sensitivity, body temperature, libido, pain threshold, and reproductive status." >>

<<leona "*No.*">>

<<system "The user cannot opt out of hormonal simulation. It is integrated at the core architecture level of the avatar." >>

<<leona "I don't — I can't — you're telling me this body is going to *cycle*? Like actually go through a month-long hormonal—">>

<<system "Correct. The simulation begins at avatar initialization and runs continuously." >>

<<system "The user is currently on Day 6 of the first cycle. Follicular phase." >>

I stared at the calendar. Day 6 was marked with a small pulsing indicator — a white dot sitting in the cool blue zone, barely a quarter of the way through. Ahead of it stretched twenty-two more days of hormonal shifts I hadn't consented to and couldn't disable, leading inexorably toward that dark red band at the end.

<<system "Phase 1: Follicular. Days 1 through 13." >>

The cool blue section of the calendar brightened. Holographic annotations appeared above each day — a rising graph line labeled ESTROGEN that climbed steadily from left to right, paired with a flat, minimal line labeled PROGESTERONE hovering near the baseline.

<<system "During the follicular phase, estrogen rises progressively. Effects include: increased energy, elevated mood stability, improved cognitive clarity, heightened skin luminosity, and gradually increasing libido." >>

<<system "This is the phase of highest baseline well-being. The user is currently experiencing it." >>

I paused. That... tracked, actually. Despite everything — the panic, the modules, the humiliation — I hadn't crashed. Not completely. I'd been frightened, furious, overwhelmed, but some baseline thread of energy had held me upright through five modules of systematic bodily education. Was that the estrogen? Was the reason I was still *functioning* because my hormonal cocktail happened to be in its generous phase?

The thought chilled me more than any of the previous modules.

<<system "Phase 2: Ovulation. Day 14." >>

The warm rose band at the center of the calendar flared. The estrogen line peaked — a sharp summit rendered in pulsing gold — and a new marker appeared: a small, luminous egg icon drifting down a holographic fallopian tube in the anatomical diagram that materialized beside the calendar.

<<system "Ovulation is the release of a mature ovum from the ovary. This is the peak fertility window." >>

<<system "Physiological effects: peak libido, heightened sensitivity across all erogenous zones, increased pheromone output, elevated body temperature by 0.3 to 0.5 degrees, and maximal cervical mucus production." >>

<<leona "Peak *libido*? How much worse does that get?">>

<<system "The user's current libido baseline is influenced by follicular estrogen. At ovulation, sexual desire intensity increases by approximately 40 to 60 percent." >>

Forty to sixty percent. I thought about Module 3 — about the jolt from a single fingertip on my clitoris, the involuntary gasp, the moisture the system had logged, the shudder I still couldn't explain. That had been Day 6. Follicular phase. The *calm* part.

At ovulation that same body would respond sixty percent harder.

<<system "Phase 3: Luteal. Days 15 through 28." >>

The calendar's right half illuminated — the gradient from rose to violet to plum. The estrogen line dropped sharply from its ovulatory peak and a second line surged upward: PROGESTERONE, rendered in deep purple, climbing to dominance.

<<system "Progesterone rises following ovulation, preparing the uterine lining for potential implantation." >>

<<system "Physiological and psychological effects vary in severity but may include:" >>

The annotations appeared one by one beside the violet gradient, stacking like a list of charges:

<<system "Mood volatility. Irritability. Anxiety. Emotional sensitivity disproportionate to stimuli." >>

<<system "Abdominal bloating. Water retention. Weight fluctuation of 1 to 2 kilograms." >>

<<system "Breast tenderness and swelling. Increased nipple sensitivity." >>

<<system "Fatigue. Reduced cognitive processing speed. Difficulty concentrating." >>

<<system "Food cravings. Altered taste perception. Nausea in severe cases." >>

<<system "Reduced pain threshold." >>

<<system "Skin oiliness and hormonal acne along the jawline." >>

<<system "Heightened emotional responses: increased crying frequency, amplified attachment behavior, lowered frustration tolerance." >>

The list kept going. Each item was a small, clinical sentence, but together they painted a portrait of two weeks spent inside a body that would betray me in ways I couldn't predict or control. My mood wouldn't be *mine*. My tears wouldn't be proportional. My breasts would swell and ache and everything I'd learned in Module 2 about their sensitivity would amplify without warning. I'd bloat. I'd crave things. I'd snap at people and not understand why until I checked which shade of purple the calendar said I was living in.

<<leona "This is... every month? All of this?">>

<<system "Every 28 days, cycling continuously until menopause or pregnancy." >>

<<system "Phase 4: Menstruation. Days 1 through 5 of the subsequent cycle." >>

The crimson band pulsed. The apartment's neon veins responded — their champagne warmth tinting toward a deep, throbbing red that beat in slow rhythm against the walls. The uterine diagram beside the calendar animated: the thick, blood-rich endometrial lining the system had spent the luteal phase building began to shed. Layer by layer. The animation was not gentle. Tissue separated from the uterine wall in fragments, mixing with blood and fluid, descending through the cervix and into the vaginal canal.

<<system "Menstruation is the shedding of the uterine lining in the absence of fertilization." >>

<<system "Duration: 3 to 7 days. Average blood loss: 30 to 80 milliliters per cycle." >>

<<system "The user will experience vaginal bleeding during this phase. Flow intensity varies by day — typically heaviest on days 2 and 3, tapering through days 4 and 5." >>

<<leona "I'm going to *bleed*. Actually bleed. From—">>

<<system "From the vaginal canal. Correct. The VR engine simulates menstrual flow with full sensory fidelity." >>

Full sensory fidelity. Meaning I wouldn't just see it. I'd *feel* it — the warmth, the wetness, the slow seep of blood from inside me pooling against whatever I was wearing or, worse, sliding down the skin of my inner thighs if I was caught unprepared. The sensation would be real. The stains would be real. The mortification would be *real*.

<<system "Menstrual management items are available at in-world vendors." >>

The wardrobe in the main room hummed, and a small holographic display materialized beside the calendar — product icons floating in a neat grid. Absorbent pads. Tampons — the diagram showed insertion technique with the same dispassionate precision as every other module. Menstrual cups. Period underwear with built-in absorption layers. Each option was annotated with capacity ratings, wear duration, and comfort scores calibrated to Leona's anatomy.

<<system "The user should acquire menstrual supplies before Day 24 of each cycle to ensure preparedness." >>

Before Day 24. Meaning I'd need to *track* this. To count the days, watch the calendar, and make sure I had products stocked before the bleeding started — because if I didn't, I'd wake up in a pool of my own blood with nothing to manage it. Planning. Forethought. The kind of quiet, invisible labor that Leo had never once considered because it had never been his to carry.

<<system "Accompanying menstruation: uterine cramping." >>

The two words landed in my stomach like stones.

<<system "Cramping is caused by prostaglandin-mediated uterine contractions that facilitate endometrial shedding." >>

<<system "Severity varies by cycle. The user's avatar is calibrated for moderate-to-severe cramp intensity based on uterine musculature and prostaglandin receptor density." >>

<<leona "Moderate to *severe*? Why would you calibrate for—">>

<<system "Immersive fidelity. The VR engine does not attenuate physiological experiences." >>

<<system "A brief demonstration will familiarize the user with menstrual cramp sensation." >>

<<leona "Wait — demonstration? No — don't you dare—">>

It hit before I finished the sentence.

Low. Deep. A grinding, twisting pressure that bloomed in the pit of my pelvis like a fist clenching around something vital. Not sharp — not a stab or a cut. Worse. A slow, relentless *wringing*, as if the muscles lining my uterus had seized and were squeezing inward from all directions, compressing the organ against itself in waves that built and receded and built again without ever fully releasing. My abdominal muscles clenched in sympathy. My lower back spasmed — a hot, aching radiation that spread from my spine outward across both hips, locking the entire region from navel to thighs in a cage of dull, heavy agony.

I doubled over. My arms wrapped around my stomach — hands pressing flat against the smooth skin below my navel, trying to push back against the pressure, achieving nothing. My thighs squeezed together. My spine curled. A sound escaped my mouth — low, involuntary, not a scream but a groan that vibrated through my clenched teeth and came out thin and miserable and *feminine*.

"Nnnhh — *god* — hhah — make it stop—"

<<system "Cramp demonstration: 40% of calibrated maximum intensity." >>

*Forty percent.* This was forty percent. This grinding, nauseating, inescapable vice in my pelvis was less than half of what my body was capable of producing every single month, and I was curled on the edge of a hovering bed with tears streaming down my cheeks unable to form words.

The cramp peaked — a long, awful plateau where every muscle from my diaphragm to my pelvic floor participated in a coordinated contraction that made me feel like my insides were being *squeezed out through a tube* — and then, mercifully, slowly, released. The tension drained. The grinding eased. My muscles unclenched one by one, from the periphery inward, until only a dull, throbbing ache remained — a ghost of the cramp, lingering like a bruise.

I was panting. Forehead pressed against my own knees. Hair hanging in a curtain around my folded body. Tears — actual tears, hot and involuntary — dripping from my lashes onto the holographic sheets.

<<system "Demonstration complete." >>

<<system "Pain management options available in-world:" >>

<<system "Analgesic potions: reduce cramp intensity by 40 to 60 percent. Available at medical vendors." >>

<<system "Thermal application: localized heat to the lower abdomen reduces uterine muscle spasm. Heat packs available at general vendors or generated by compatible furniture." >>

The hovering bed hummed beneath me — and a warm patch materialized against my lower belly where I was pressed into the mattress. Gentle, targeted heat radiating directly into the muscles that had just finished trying to wring themselves dry. The relief was immediate — not complete, but significant. The dull ache softened. The lingering tension in my lower back eased. My breathing slowed.

<<system "Positioning: lateral recumbent with knees drawn toward chest reduces pelvic floor tension and alleviates cramp pressure." >>

The bed demonstrated — a holographic outline showing a figure curled on her side, knees bent, pillow between her thighs. Fetal position, essentially. The recovery pose of every woman who'd ever been ambushed by her own uterus.

<<leona "Can I... turn it off?">>

My voice came out wrecked. Barely a whisper. Hoarse from the groan I hadn't been able to suppress.

<<leona "The cycle. The cramps. All of it. Can I disable the hormonal simulation?">>

<<system "Hormonal cycle simulation is a core avatar function. It cannot be disabled, paused, or attenuated." >>

<<leona "There has to be a setting — a preference — *something* — you can't force someone to go through this every month without any way to—">>

<<system "The cycle is integrated into the avatar's endocrine, reproductive, neurological, and emotional systems. Disabling it would require disabling the avatar." >>

<<system "The user may manage symptoms through in-world medical items, behavioral adaptation, and cycle tracking." >>

<<system "The user may not eliminate the cycle itself." >>

The calendar still stretched across the windows — that long, inevitable gradient from blue calm through rose heat through violet descent into crimson. Twenty-eight days. Repeating. Forever. Or at least for as long as I wore this body, which the system seemed to consider the same thing.

I uncurled. Slowly. The heated patch on the bed followed my movement, tracking the warmth against my abdomen as I shifted onto my back and stared at the ceiling. Chrome. Smooth. The reflection of a girl lying with her knees drawn up and her hands resting on her lower belly, cradling the ache that lingered where a cramp had demonstrated — at forty percent — what awaited her at the end of every cycle.

<<system "Cycle tracking is displayed on the user's biometric interface, accessible at any time." >>

A small icon appeared in my peripheral vision — a tiny crescent moon in the upper left of my visual field, rendered in the pale blue of Day 6. It would change color as the days passed. Blue to teal to rose to violet to red. A countdown I could never escape, ticking in the corner of my eye.

<<system "Module 6 complete." >>

The crimson light in the neon veins receded. The calendar faded from the windows, and Eidolon City returned — its skyline now fully shifted to artificial dawn, amber and gold streaming between the towers, holographic advertisements flickering to life on distant facades. A new day in a city I still hadn't set foot in, seen through windows I still hadn't looked through without flinching.

The small crescent moon glowed in my peripheral vision. Day 6. Twenty-two days until crimson.

I pressed my palms flat against my lower abdomen — still warm from the bed's heat pad, still tender from the echo of a contraction that had lasted maybe fifteen seconds and broken me completely — and tried to imagine enduring the real thing. Days of it. While bleeding. While mood-swinging. While my breasts ached and my skin broke out and my crying threshold dropped to nothing.

Every month.

<<system "Module 7: Pregnancy Risk and Reproductive Function." >>

<<system "Beginning in sixty seconds." >>

I kept my hands on my stomach. Felt the warmth of my own skin. The gentle rise and fall of breathing. The subtle, impossible awareness of something *inside* me — not a presence, not a feeling, just a knowledge that hadn't existed twelve hours ago. The knowledge that my body contained a space designed to grow a life, and the system was about to teach me everything that implied.

Sixty seconds.

I didn't move. I didn't close my eyes. I just lay there on the hovering bed with the city's golden light painting my skin and the crescent moon counting the days in the corner of my vision and my hands resting on a belly that the system had filled with organs Leo had never needed and Leona could never escape.

[[Continue|day_4_38_les]]


:: day_4_38_les

I didn't sit up when the module began. I couldn't. The ghost of that cramp demonstration still throbbed beneath my palms — a dull, residual ache pulsing in the muscles the system had seized and released like a stress test on an engine — and the thought of standing, of facing another holographic dissection of my own interior while upright and exposed, was more than my legs were willing to negotiate.

So Module 7 came to me.

The ceiling became the display.

The chrome surface above the bed shimmered, its reflective polish dissolving into a projection field that spread outward from directly above my face until the entire ceiling was a canvas. And on that canvas — rendered in luminous, slowly rotating, devastatingly detailed holographic anatomy — my reproductive system materialized.

Not the cross-section of Module 3. Not a clinical diagram with labeled arrows. This was a *living* simulation. Full three-dimensional depth. Organs that pulsed, contracted, and shifted in real-time biological rhythm, hovering above me as if I'd been opened up and the system was showing me what it had placed inside.

The uterus floated at the center — pear-shaped, fist-sized, its muscular walls rendered in layers of translucent rose-pink that contracted in slow, rhythmic waves I could *see* even though the contractions were imperceptible to conscious sensation. The system had built it to move on its own. To work without my knowledge or permission.

Branching from either side, the fallopian tubes curved outward in elegant, finger-fringed arcs — the fimbriae at their open ends swaying gently, like sea anemones tasting the water. And nestled at the terminus of each tube, cradled in holographic ligament, sat an ovary. Small. Almond-shaped. Dense with follicles rendered as tiny, luminous spheres clustered beneath the surface, each one containing —

<<system "Module 7: Pregnancy Risk and Reproductive Function." >>

<<system "The user's avatar is equipped with a fully functional reproductive system." >>

<<leona "Fully functional meaning what, exactly.">>

I knew the answer. I'd known it since Module 6 had shown me the calendar. But hearing it was different from knowing it, the same way watching a cliff approach was different from falling.

<<system "Fully functional meaning the avatar is capable of conception, gestation, and parturition." >>

<<leona "Partu — that's — that means *giving birth*.">>

<<system "Correct." >>

The hologram zoomed to the left ovary. One follicle — larger than the others, pressing against the ovarian surface like a bubble about to burst — pulsed with a warm golden light.

<<system "Each cycle, the dominant follicle matures and releases a single ovum during ovulation." >>

The simulation animated. The golden follicle swelled, thinned, and ruptured in slow motion — a tiny sphere of light drifting free from the ovarian surface and tumbling into the waiting fimbriae of the fallopian tube. The finger-like projections caught it. Swept it inward. The ovum began its journey down the tube — a slow, luminous migration toward the uterus, carried by peristaltic contractions and the current of cilia lining the inner walls.

<<system "The ovum remains viable for approximately 12 to 24 hours following release." >>

<<system "This is the fertilization window." >>

The calendar from Module 6 reappeared — a smaller version, projected alongside the anatomy — and Day 14 blazed gold. But the system expanded the window: Days 11 through 16 highlighted in a pulsing amber gradient.

<<system "However, sperm cells survive within the female reproductive tract for up to five days." >>

<<system "Therefore, the effective conception window extends from approximately Day 11 through Day 16 of the cycle." >>

<<system "Intercourse during this window carries a probability of fertilization." >>

The hologram demonstrated. A second animation — spermatozoa appearing in the vaginal canal, rendered as a swarm of blue-white points of light surging upward through the cervix, into the uterus, funneling into the fallopian tubes. Thousands of points. Then hundreds. Then dozens. Then a single persistent light reaching the golden ovum where it drifted in the tube's ampulla.

Contact.

The two lights merged. The golden sphere flared — doubling in brightness, tripling — and the system tracked its descent down the remaining length of the fallopian tube in accelerated time. Six days compressed into six seconds. The fertilized ovum divided. Two cells. Four. Eight. A hollow ball of light — blastocyst, the label read — that drifted into the uterine cavity and nestled against the thickened endometrial lining the luteal phase had spent two weeks constructing.

Implantation.

The blastocyst burrowed into the uterine wall. The lining closed over it. The progesterone line on the calendar didn't drop the way it had in Module 6's menstrual demonstration — it *climbed*, sustained by a new signal from the embedded embryo, telling the body: *don't shed. Don't bleed. I'm here. Keep building.*

<<system "Fertilization and implantation initiate pregnancy." >>

<<system "The VR engine will simulate pregnancy to full term unless terminated through in-world medical intervention." >>

I sat up. The anatomy tracked me — the ceiling projection adjusting its angle as I rose, keeping the holographic uterus centered above me, the implanted blastocyst pulsing softly in its wall like a buried star.

<<leona "Simulate to full term. You mean — the whole thing. Nine months.">>

<<system "Forty weeks. Correct." >>

<<system "Pregnancy simulation includes:" >>

The annotations appeared beside the uterus — a cascading list that built itself line by line, each entry more impossible than the last.

<<system "Progressive hormonal changes. First trimester: elevated hCG causing nausea, fatigue, breast tenderness, mood volatility, and aversion to certain stimuli." >>

<<system "Anatomical changes. The uterus will expand from fist-sized to approximately 35 centimeters in length. Abdominal distension will be visible and progressive." >>

The hologram demonstrated — the uterus expanding in accelerated time, rising from the pelvis into the abdominal cavity, displacing organs, stretching the abdominal wall. A ghostly silhouette of Leona's profile appeared beside it, showing the belly growing in monthly increments. Flat. Then a subtle curve. Then a visible swell. Then unmistakable. Then enormous — a full, taut dome of stretched skin and displaced muscle that pushed the breasts upward and the spine into a compensatory curve.

<<leona "Oh my god.">>

<<system "Breast changes. Cup size increase of 1 to 2 sizes. Areolar darkening. Onset of lactation capability in the third trimester." >>

<<system "Weight gain. 10 to 15 kilograms distributed across the uterus, fetus, placenta, amniotic fluid, expanded blood volume, and adipose stores." >>

<<system "Pelvic joint loosening. Ligament laxity caused by relaxin hormone, increasing flexibility for delivery but reducing joint stability." >>

<<system "Cardiovascular changes. Blood volume increase of 40 to 50 percent. Elevated heart rate. Potential for edema in the extremities." >>

<<system "Fetal movement. Perceptible to the user from approximately week 18. Increasing in intensity through the third trimester." >>

<<leona "I would *feel* it? Moving?">>

<<system "The VR engine simulates fetal movement with full haptic fidelity. Kicks, rolls, and hiccups are rendered as internal tactile sensations calibrated to gestational stage." >>

I stared at the expanded holographic uterus — now the size of a watermelon above me, its walls stretched thin, a small curled shape visible within the amniotic space. Not detailed. Not a recognizable infant. Just a suggestion. A presence. A passenger that my body had been designed to carry whether or not I ever invited one aboard.

<<system "Culminating in labor and delivery." >>

<<leona "Stop.">>

<<system "Labor simulation includes uterine contractions of escalating intensity and frequency, cervical dilation from 0 to 10 centimeters—" >>

<<leona "*Stop*. I get it. I *get* it.">>

The hologram paused. The expanded uterus hung above me — frozen mid-contraction, the muscular walls caught in a squeezing motion that made the cramp demonstration from Module 6 look like a gentle hug. The system waited three seconds, then resumed at a lower intensity.

<<system "The user is advised that pregnancy in Eidolon carries full experiential weight. The simulation does not abbreviate or attenuate." >>

<<system "Proceeding to contraception." >>

The uterus shrank back to its normal size. The implanted blastocyst vanished. The calendar dimmed to its baseline colors. And a new display materialized on the windows — a vendor catalog rendered in the aesthetic language of Eidolon City: cyberpunk-styled product icons floating in a neon-edged grid.

<<system "Barrier methods." >>

The first category illuminated. Icons appeared: items that looked like they belonged in a sci-fi pharmacy — sleek, translucent sheaths labeled INTERNAL BARRIER, compact capsules labeled CERVICAL SEAL, and a familiar shape rendered in holographic chrome labeled EXTERNAL BARRIER — PARTNER USE.

<<system "Internal barriers are inserted into the vaginal canal prior to intercourse and prevent sperm from reaching the cervix. Efficacy: 88 to 94 percent with correct use." >>

<<system "Cervical seals are applied directly to the cervix and dissolve after 12 hours. Efficacy: 91 percent." >>

<<system "External barriers are worn by the penetrating partner. Efficacy: 95 to 98 percent with correct use." >>

<<leona "Ninety-eight. Not a hundred.">>

<<system "No contraceptive method achieves 100 percent efficacy." >>

The second category lit up. Hormonal options — and here the Eidolon aesthetic took over completely. No pills or patches. Instead: luminous potions in crystal vials labeled CYCLE SUPPRESSANT, glowing subdermal implants shaped like slivers of neon light labeled FERTILITY LOCK — 90 DAY, and an ornate injector pen labeled QUARTERLY HORMONAL.

<<system "Hormonal contraceptives suppress ovulation, preventing follicular release. Efficacy: 96 to 99 percent depending on method and adherence." >>

<<system "Cycle suppressants are consumed orally. Duration: 24 hours per dose. Missed doses reduce efficacy significantly." >>

<<system "Fertility locks are subdermal implants lasting 90 days. Inserted at medical vendors. Highest long-term efficacy: 99.2 percent." >>

<<system "Quarterly hormonal injections are administered at medical clinics. Efficacy: 98.7 percent. May cause mood fluctuation and breakthrough bleeding." >>

<<leona "Do the hormonal ones stop the period too?">>

<<system "Some hormonal methods reduce or eliminate menstrual bleeding as a secondary effect. The cycle suppressant maintains the hormonal cycle but prevents ovulation. The fertility lock suppresses both ovulation and menstruation in approximately 60 percent of users." >>

That number hung in the air. Sixty percent. A sixty percent chance of no bleeding, no cramps, no crimson band on the calendar. I filed it away with a desperation that bordered on hunger.

<<system "Emergency contraception." >>

A final icon appeared — a single vial of bright amber liquid, pulsing urgently.

<<system "Emergency contraceptive potion. Effective within 72 hours of unprotected intercourse. Delays or prevents ovulation if taken before the LH surge. Efficacy: 85 to 89 percent, decreasing with time elapsed." >>

<<system "Available at medical vendors and emergency stations throughout Eidolon City." >>

<<system "This is a last-resort measure. Not intended for regular use. Side effects include severe nausea, hormonal disruption, and irregular bleeding for up to two weeks." >>

The vendor catalog faded. The holographic anatomy returned — smaller now, resting at eye level in front of me like a patient waiting for acknowledgment. The uterus pulsed. The ovaries glowed. The fallopian tubes swayed.

<<system "Summary: the user's avatar is reproductively functional at all times. Unprotected intercourse during the fertile window carries conception risk. Pregnancy, if initiated, simulates to full term with complete physiological and experiential fidelity." >>

<<system "Contraception is the user's responsibility." >>

<<leona "Can I disable it? The reproductive function. Just — turn it off. Make this body infertile.">>

<<system "Reproductive function is a core avatar architecture." >>

<<leona "I know what you're going to say—">>

<<system "It cannot be disabled, paused, or attenuated." >>

<<leona "There has to be some kind of admin override — a developer setting — *something*—">>

<<system "The user's avatar was generated from a subconscious emotional resonance imprint. All core systems — hormonal, reproductive, neurological — are integrated at the foundational level. Removing any core system would destabilize the avatar beyond operational parameters." >>

<<system "The user is fertile. The world will not pretend otherwise." >>

The hologram held for three more seconds — the ovaries glowing, the uterus pulsing, the fallopian tubes open and waiting — then dissolved into soft motes of light that drifted downward and vanished against the chrome floor.

I sat on the edge of the bed, legs hanging, bare feet not quite touching the floor. The city beyond the windows was fully dawn-lit now — golden towers, honey-colored haze, the distant sparkle of hover-traffic threading between buildings like luminous blood through capillaries. Beautiful. Alive. A world built for adventure and exploration and possibility.

And I'd be walking into it with a body that could get pregnant.

Not as an abstract risk. Not as a theoretical scenario. As a biological *fact*, ticking away inside me with the same quiet certainty as the crescent moon counting days in the corner of my vision. Ovaries producing eggs. A uterus building and shedding and building again. A cervix that opened and closed on its own schedule. A vaginal canal that led somewhere — *inward*, to a space that could nurture or could bleed, depending on what reached it and when.

I looked down at my bare belly. Smooth. Flat. The gentle rise of a lower abdomen that contained everything the system had just shown me. I pressed my palm against it — low, below the navel, where the uterus sat behind muscle and skin. I couldn't feel it. Not directly. But I knew it was there now with a certainty that changed the shape of every thought I'd have from this moment forward.

Every encounter. Every flirtation. Every fight. Every moment of vulnerability in a world I didn't understand yet — all of it would carry this weight. This *possibility*. That the body I wore could be changed by something as simple as timing and proximity and a failure of prevention.

<<system "Module 7 complete." >>

The golden dawn light painted long warm streaks across the chrome floor. The neon veins settled into their resting rhythm. The calendar icon — that small crescent moon — glowed softly in my peripheral vision. Day 6. Eight days until the gold zone. Eight days until the window the system had just spent an entire module teaching me to fear.

<<system "Module 8: Arousal Response and Sexual Function." >>

<<system "Beginning in sixty seconds." >>

I pressed both hands flat against my lower belly and held them there. Feeling the warmth of my own skin. The subtle rhythm of my breath. The quiet, impossible knowledge of what lived beneath my palms.

Sixty seconds until the system taught me how this body wanted.

I wasn't sure I was ready to know.

But the system hadn't asked what I was ready for since the moment I put on the headset, and it wasn't going to start now.

[[Continue|day_4_39_les]]

:: day_4_39_les

The sixty seconds felt like a countdown to an execution.

I spent them lying on my back with my hands still pressed against my lower belly, staring at the chrome ceiling where Module 7's holographic uterus had floated minutes ago. The surface was blank now — just brushed metal reflecting the dawn-gold light from the windows — but I could still see the ghost of it. The ovaries. The tubes. The little golden egg drifting toward a destination I couldn't lock shut.

When the apartment shifted, it shifted *warm*.

Not the clinical blue-white of the anatomy modules. Not the amber of the bathroom. Not the champagne of hygiene or the crimson of menstruation. This was something else — a low, diffuse radiance that rose from the neon veins like heat from sun-warmed stone. Rose-gold. The same frequency the tracking beams had used in Module 2 when they'd traced my breasts. The same frequency, I realized with a dropping stomach, the system associated with *sensation*.

The pulse-lights in the chrome floor activated in a new pattern — slow, rolling waves that traveled from the periphery of the room toward the center where I lay, each wave slightly warmer than the last, as if the apartment was breathing inward. Toward me. The hovering bed's magnetic hum dropped a register, becoming a low, thrumming vibration that I felt in my spine, my hips, the backs of my thighs.

<<system "Module 8: Arousal Response and Sexual Function." >>

I closed my eyes.

<<leona "Skip it.">>

<<system "Module 8 is mandatory." >>

<<leona "I already — Module 3 covered the anatomy. I know where everything is. I know the nerve counts. I don't need—">>

<<system "Module 3 covered anatomical identification. Module 8 covers physiological response — the arousal cycle, orgasmic pathways, and sexual function as an integrated system." >>

<<leona "Please. I am *asking* you. Skip this one.">>

<<system "The guide will not advance until Module 8 is complete. Logout remains disabled." >>

<<system "Understanding the user's sexual response system prevents injury, dysphoria during involuntary arousal, and psychological distress during intimate encounters." >>

<<leona "I'm not going to *have* intimate encounters! I'm going to log out the second this guide ends and never put the headset on again!">>

The system paused. One full second of silence — the longest deliberate pause it had taken since the guide began. Then:

<<system "The user's elevated heart rate, vasodilation pattern, and genital blood flow during Module 3 suggest that intimate encounters — voluntary or involuntary, anticipated or spontaneous — will produce intense physiological responses that the user is not currently equipped to understand or manage." >>

<<system "Module 8 equips the user." >>

<<system "Proceeding." >>

