Not the warm, forgiving amber of the bathroom or the soft gold of Eidolon's dawn. This was deeper. Heavier. The color of hazard lights on industrial machinery — a saturated, cautionary tone that seeped into the neon veins like dye injected into a vascular system. The walls darkened around it. The pulse-lights in the chrome floor extinguished entirely, leaving the room lit only by that slow, heavy amber bleeding from the walls and ceiling in rhythmic waves that pulsed at a frequency lower than my heartbeat.

The apartment felt different. Every previous module had reshaped the room's atmosphere — clinical, domestic, warm, educational — but this was the first time the space felt like a *warning*. The air itself seemed denser. The hovering bed's magnetic hum dropped to its lowest register, almost subliminal, a vibration felt in the bones rather than heard. The windows remained transparent, Eidolon's skyline visible beyond, but the threat-assessment overlay from Module 9 had reactivated unbidden — the color-coded map painting the city in green, yellow, and red, the red zones pulsing now in sync with the room's amber heartbeat.

